# THE ROOT — what a verse looks like, by top-level path.
#
# This is the one part of the schema that no Go type declares. Every other definition is the
# wire shape of a struct somewhere (generated) or a correction to one (curated.tony); this one
# says what the surface IS: the top-level paths, and the shape served at each.
#
# It is HAND-WRITTEN, and that is the trade. It used to be derived from versed's route table,
# which made it unable to lie and also made the whole schema unobtainable without a server —
# you could not read what a verse looks like without running one. Now the schema stands alone
# and the API is what has to agree with it, which is a test (TestTheRootIsTheServedSurface)
# rather than a construction.
#
# A key is a BARE top-level path — entities, not flow/tree or processes/{id}/inbox. One level
# down belongs to the route table at GET /api, which is the complete surface; this is the root.
#
# What is missing, and why:
#
#   gates   — its rows are still assembled as map literals, so there is no named shape to point
#             at. Typing it `object` would be a claim true of anything. It appears here the day
#             its handler answers in a named type.
#   watch   — a stream of server-sent events, not a document. The frames carry verse-event, but
#             the WIRE form of the stream is not described yet; that is worth adding, and this
#             is where it goes when it is.
#   schema  — this document's own shape, which is likewise not a named type.
#
# The control plane is absent by construction: POST /flow/pause is something you DO to a verse,
# not part of what one looks like.
context:
- tony-format/context
define:
  api: .[verse-routes]
  entities: .[array(verse-entity)]
  entity: .[verse-entity]
  flows: .[array(string)]
  goals: .[array(verse-goal)]
  processes: .[array(verse-instance)]
  status: .[verse-status]
  strata: .[array(verse-stratum)]
  triggers: .[array(verse-trigger)]
signature:
  name: root
